### Technical Breakdown: Thread Priorities & Preemptive Scheduling in Java

#### 1. Architecture of Thread Scheduling:
Java uses **Preemptive Priority-Based Scheduling**:
- At any moment, the scheduler selects the highest-priority runnable thread for CPU execution.
- If a thread with higher priority becomes runnable, the running lower-priority thread is preempted.
- Threads with equal priority are scheduled via **Round-Robin** time-slicing.

#### 2. Priority Levels & Platform Mapping:
| Constant Field | Integer Value | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`Thread.MIN_PRIORITY`** | **1** | Lowest scheduling preference (background logging, cleanup). |
| **`Thread.NORM_PRIORITY`** | **5** | Standard default priority for all application worker threads. |
| **`Thread.MAX_PRIORITY`** | **10** | Critical UI or real-time event dispatcher threads. |

> **Important Caveat:** Java thread priorities map to native OS thread priorities. Because Windows, Linux, and macOS prioritize native threads differently, relying strictly on thread priorities for program correctness is discouraged; use explicit synchronization primitives instead.

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation:
```java
class PriorityWorker extends Thread {
    private long counter = 0;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public PriorityWorker(String name, int priority) {
        super(name);
        setPriority(priority);
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        while (running) {
            counter++;
        }
        System.out.printf("[%s] (Priority %2d) completed with count: %,d\n", 
                          getName(), getPriority(), counter);
    }

    public void stopWorker() {
        this.running = false;
    }
}

public class ThreadPriorityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        PriorityWorker low = new PriorityWorker("Low-Priority-Worker", Thread.MIN_PRIORITY);
        PriorityWorker med = new PriorityWorker("Med-Priority-Worker", Thread.NORM_PRIORITY);
        PriorityWorker high = new PriorityWorker("High-Priority-Worker", Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);

        low.start();
        med.start();
        high.start();

        Thread.sleep(1000); // Allow threads to contend for CPU time for 1 second

        low.stopWorker();
        med.stopWorker();
        high.stopWorker();

        low.join();
        med.join();
        high.join();
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Thread priorities optimize CPU slice allocation across competing concurrent threads.}$$
